 1 big thing: SpaceX Starship goes orbital 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Starship launches from Starbase, Texas, this morning. Photo: Ronaldo Schemidt/AFP via Getty Images SpaceX's next-generation Starship rocket reached Earth orbit today for the first time before being brought home early, Alex Fitzpatrick reports. 

 An explosion was visible on the company's livestream just after Starship touched down in the Pacific Ocean. The cause remains unclear. 🚀 The massive, fully reusable Starship is key to SpaceX's commercial spaceflight ambitions.

 NASA is counting on a modified version for use as a lunar lander in its Artemis program. Starship launched from South Texas just before 9 a.m. ET on a planned test flight of about 10 hours.

 It was set to orbit Earth about six times before splashing down in the Pacific Ocean, west of Chile. But SpaceX decided to bring it back to Earth after only about three hours. As part of the mission, Starship deployed 26 of the company's new, better-performing Starlink V3 internet satellites. Crowds watch this morning's Starship launch. Photo: Ronaldo Schemidt/AFP via Getty Images 🌎 SpaceX said in a mission summary: "Flight tests until this point have intentionally flown passively safe suborbital trajectories to maximize public safety while allowing for maximum learning."

 "By going to orbit, the next phase of developing Starship to be fully and rapidly reusable can begin." What's next: SpaceX plans to eventually "catch" Starship in the arms of its launch tower — a feat it's accomplished with Super Heavy, but not Starship — but didn't make the attempt this time.

